<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Chrysoloras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1350–<a href="/years/1415">1415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Chrysoloras was a Byzantine scholar and diplomat who taught Greek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1397, the first sustained teaching of the language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for centuries. His pupils included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a> and Guarino da Verona, and his grammar, the Erotemata, was widely used. His teaching opened classical Greek literature to the Italian humanists. He died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> in 1415.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras.jpg" alt="Manuel Chrysoloras" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Uccello. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel-chrysoloras-paolo-uccello-louvre-15th.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel Chrysoloras was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> into a distinguished Greek Orthodox family. In 1390 he led an embassy from the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-ii-palaiologos">Manuel II Palaiologos</a> to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, asking the aid of the Christian princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> against the invasions of the Ottoman Turks. In Venice he met Roberto de' Rossi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and in 1395 Rossi's acquaintance Jacopo d'Angelo went to Constantinople to study Greek with him. In 1396 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coluccio-salutati">Coluccio Salutati</a>, the Chancellor of Florence, invited him to Florence to teach Greek grammar and literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Chrysoloras arrived in the winter of 1397. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a>, one of his most well-known pupils, remembered it as a great opportunity: there were many teachers of law, but no one in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had studied Greek for 700 years. He taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1397 to 1400, beginning with the rudiments, and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. A close-knit group of chosen students became among the first humanists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, including Bruni, d'Angelo, Ambrogio Traversari, Guarino da Verona, Niccolò de' Niccoli, Carlo Marsuppini, Pier Paolo Vergerio, Uberto Decembrio and Palla Strozzi.</p><p>He later visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Pavia, lived for several years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at the invitation of Bruni, then secretary to Pope Gregory XII. In 1408 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on a mission for Manuel II, and in 1413 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to the Holy Roman Emperor Sigismund, to fix a place for the church council that later met at Constance. He was chosen to represent the Greek Church there when he died suddenly on 15 April 1415. Guarino da Verona gathered the commemorative essays his death prompted in the Chrysolorina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Chrysoloras translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-republic">Plato's Republic</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. His Erotemata, or Questions, was the first basic Greek grammar in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>; it was first published in 1484 and widely reprinted, and Thomas Linacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and Desiderius Erasmus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> studied it at once. His Three Letters on the Comparison of Old and New Rome compared Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He was chiefly influential through his teaching, which made men such as Bruni, Salutati and Guarino familiar with the masterpieces of ancient Greek literature and philosophy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel Chrysoloras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scholars such as <b>Manuel Chrysoloras</b> carried it to Italy from 1397, as did émigrés after the empire's fall, and it helped shape the Italian Renaissance: the recovery of Plato and the Greek historians in Florence, Venice and Rome owed much to Byzantine scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its revival began when the Byzantine scholar <b>Manuel Chrysoloras</b> began teaching in Florence in 1397; Greek scholars brought manuscripts to Italy before and after the fall of Constantinople in 1453; the printer Aldus Manutius published the Greek classics in Venice; and Erasmus published the Greek New Testament in…</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Coluccio Salutati, chancellor of the Republic of Florence from 1375 to 1406, gathered younger scholars around him and in 1397 brought the Byzantine diplomat <b>Manuel Chrysoloras</b> to teach Greek; among those who learned from him was Leonardo Bruni, who went on to translate Plato and Aristotle into Latin.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1392" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manuel Chrysoloras"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1392">What was here in 1392? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
